<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- pinion §5.38 §5.39 R1569 — KeySequenceEdit widget statechart
     (Qt `QKeySequenceEdit`).

     The widget a keymap-preferences pane is built from: it displays a
     chord sequence and, while RECORDING, takes every keystroke away
     from the window's accelerator layers so that a chord which already
     means something can still be recorded as itself.

     States: idle / recording / disabled.

     - **idle**: showing a sequence, claiming nothing. The window's
       mnemonics and `keybinding` map work normally. `record` enters
       `recording`; `disable` enters `disabled`.

     - **recording**: every chord reaches this widget first
       (`External::shadows_accelerator` answers `true` in this state and
       only in this state). Three exits, and the fact that there are
       three is the point:
         * `commit` -> `idle` with `keysequence.captured` raised — the
           sequence is accepted. Driven by the sequence reaching its
           declared maximum length, or by the user stopping deliberately.
         * `cancel` -> `idle` silently — the sequence in flight is
           discarded and the previous one kept.
         * `blur` -> `idle` with `keysequence.captured` raised. The toolkit's
           `focusOutEvent` calls `finishEditing()`, so focus loss
           ACCEPTING what was typed is Qt's behaviour and is kept.

     - **disabled**: absorbing class until `enable` arrives. A disabled
       widget claims no chord, for the same reason a read-only
       `QLineEdit` declines `ShortcutOverride`: a widget that will not
       act on the key must not stop the accelerator that would have.

     Why `record` is an EXPLICIT event, where Qt has none:

     Qt starts recording on focus-in and stops only on focus-out or a
     1-second release timer. That leaves a Qt keymap editor with no way
     to stop grabbing while it still has focus, and no way to start
     deliberately — so a user who tabs into the field and then presses
     the application's Save shortcut silently overwrites the binding
     they were looking at. Making the arm an event is what lets the
     shadow be a function of a state the user chose, and it is what
     every real keymap editor (KDE `KKeySequenceWidget`, and the
     record-button pattern in VS Code / IntelliJ) does instead.

     `cancel` likewise has no Qt counterpart: `QKeySequenceEdit` cannot
     abandon an in-flight recording, because the release timer commits
     whatever arrived.

     Vocabulary notes:

     * `disable` / `enable` mirror the ScrollBar (R55.D.2), Slider
       (R51.14) and TextField (R56.1.a) vocabulary — the closed-core
       §5.13 enable/disable pair shared across the widget catalogue.
     * `blur` mirrors the W3C DOM `FocusEvent` naming §5.39 focus
       dispatch uses, exactly as `text_field.scxml` does.
     * `keysequence.captured` is the §5.20 intent raise, the same shape
       `textfield.commit` has.
-->
<scxml xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/07/scxml"
       version="1.0"
       datamodel="null"
       initial="idle">

  <state id="idle">
    <transition event="record" target="recording"/>
    <transition event="disable" target="disabled"/>
  </state>

  <state id="recording">
    <transition event="commit" target="idle">
      <raise event="keysequence.captured"/>
    </transition>
    <transition event="cancel" target="idle"/>
    <transition event="blur" target="idle">
      <raise event="keysequence.captured"/>
    </transition>
    <transition event="disable" target="disabled"/>
  </state>

  <state id="disabled">
    <transition event="enable" target="idle"/>
  </state>

</scxml>
